        <div id="incline-foldable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The RHYTHM FUN Incline Foldable Treadmill, Explained</h2>
<p>
            Many people search for the “RHYTHM FUN incline foldable treadmill”, which is the start of the
            title RHYTHM FUN has used on Amazon for its incline walking pads with a handle bar. Reseller
            sites that copy Amazon listings carry it as “RHYTHM FUN Incline Foldable Treadmill, Walking Pad
            with Handle Bar”, describing a three-level manual incline to 8%, while the same listing’s
            specification table gives a maximum of 6%. We could not open the Amazon listing itself to see
            which model it now sells, and RHYTHM FUN’s store did not list an 8% walking pad on
            5 November 2026; its only 8% machine was the S8 treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            All of RHYTHM FUN’s inclines are manual. On a walking pad that means you lift the front of the
            deck and set it on a fold-out foot or lever at one of two or three fixed positions, with the
            belt stopped. It is simpler and cheaper than a motorised incline, and nothing in it can burn
            out, but you cannot change the gradient mid-walk, and the stated percentage is the deck angle
            at the highest position, not a range you can choose within. Our guides to <a href="/what-does-incline-walking-do/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline walking does</a> and <a href="/incline-walking-vs-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline walking vs. running</a> explain why even a modest gradient makes a difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            “Foldable” also means different things across the range. The walking pads fold their handlebars
            flat so the whole pad slides under a sofa or bed, at a folded height of 3.9 to 5 inches as
            RHYTHM FUN gives it. The 3.0 HP treadmill folds its handles down and rolls on wheels. Neither
            folds in half the way some compact pads do. Our <a href="/walking-pad-vs-folding-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs. folding treadmill</a> guide explains the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="discontinued" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Happened to the RHYTHM FUN AP01 and AP02?</h2>
<p>
            The AP01 and AP02 were the RHYTHM FUN pads most reviewed on Amazon: small, quiet incline walking
            pads with a 300 lb rating and a one-year warranty, the AP01 with an 8% manual incline and the
            AP02, lighter, with about 3.5%. By February 2026 OCDevel’s tracker recorded that both had been
            removed from Amazon US, that the AP02’s page on RHYTHM FUN’s store returned an error, and that
            the AP01 was still on the store at $229.99. On 5 November 2026 neither appeared in RHYTHM FUN’s
            catalogue at all. The tracker also recorded the CP03, a 15% incline pad, which was not on the
            store either.
          </p>
          <p>
            Even the tracker’s records for these pads carry conflicts: the AP01’s top speed appears as both
            3.7 and 3.8 mph and its weight as 40 and 48 lb, and the AP02’s incline as 3.5% and as three
            unspecified levels. If you find one for sale, it is old stock or second-hand; check it runs
            smoothly at full speed and incline before paying, and our guide to <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a used treadmill</a> covers what else to look for. If you own one, RHYTHM FUN’s support should still honour the
            warranty within its year, and the <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad lubrication guide</a> applies as for any pad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Check Before Buying a RHYTHM FUN</h2>
<ol>
            <li><strong>Which model you are getting.</strong> RHYTHM FUN’s titles are long and similar. Match
            the <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>incline percentage</a>, handlebar style, top speed and belt length to one of the cards above.</li>
            <li><strong>Who sells and ships it.</strong> On Amazon, check “Ships from” and “Sold by”. On
            RHYTHM FUN’s store, the brand is the seller.</li>
            <li><strong>The weight limit, with a margin.</strong> RHYTHM FUN gives 300 lb for its pads.
            Leave at least 50 lb of margin; our <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a> explains why.</li>
            <li><strong>The price against the YPOO equivalent.</strong> Search for a YPOO pad with the same
            photos and figures; you may find the same machine for less.</li>
            <li><strong>Recent low-star reviews.</strong> Read them for belt slipping, the incline foot
            loosening, the remote failing and slow support, and make sure their photos show the same model.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a> page explains what changes with the seller.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy a RHYTHM FUN Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A RHYTHM FUN is a reasonable buy</strong> if you want a compact incline walking pad with
            handlebars for under $210, you weigh well under 300 lb, and you will mostly walk. The 13% pad is
            the one whose figures we could check, and the one we would choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you are tall, heavy or want to run, since every RHYTHM FUN has a
            belt of about 37.5 inches; if you want a motorised incline; or if you want more than a year of
            warranty. Our <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with incline</a> and <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with handrails</a> roundups cover both types, and our <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a> roundup covers machines with longer decks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, set it up so it lasts. Put the pad on a firm, level surface or a <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill mat</a>, not deep carpet, so the motor housing can breathe and the deck does not flex.
            Check the belt is centred and not slipping after the first few hours, when new belts stretch,
            and lubricate it on the schedule in the manual. Fold the handlebars down and lift the incline
            foot only with the belt stopped. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-make-a-cheap-treadmill-last-longer/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making a cheap treadmill last longer</a> covers the rest, and most of it costs nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a RHYTHM FUN Walking Pad</h2>
          <p>These are the errors we see most often with RHYTHM FUN and similar budget incline pads, and each has a simple fix.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting a motorised incline</h3>
              <p>RHYTHM FUN’s inclines are manual and fixed. Decide which gradient you want before you start walking, because changing it means stopping and lifting the deck.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying the higher of two prices</h3>
              <p>RHYTHM FUN listed one treadmill twice at $269.99 and $399.99. Check for duplicates, and for the same machine under another name, before you pay.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Running on a 37-inch belt</h3>
              <p>A 6.2 or 7.5 mph top speed is an allowance, not an invitation. On a belt this short, keep to walking and gentle jogging.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a discontinued AP model unseen</h3>
              <p>Old-stock or used AP01 and AP02 pads may have worn belts or motors. Watch one run at full speed before you buy, or buy a current model.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">RHYTHM FUN’s 13% incline walking pad with side handlebars, at $209.99,
            is a reasonable budget pad for incline desk walking</strong>, and the one current model whose
            main figures an independent source confirms. The rest of the range rests on RHYTHM FUN’s word,
            the AP01 and AP02 are gone, and the warranty is one year.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep to walking and light jogging, leave a margin under the 300 lb limit, and compare the price
            with any identical YPOO pad. For a longer belt and checked figures, the MERACH W50 TrekPad, or
            the pads in our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best walking pads</a> roundup.
          </p>
        </div>

